1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,064
Near the top of Taipei one oh

2
00:00:02,064 --> 00:00:04,626
one, one of the tallest buildings in

3
00:00:04,626 --> 00:00:07,189
the world, hangs a golden steel ball

4
00:00:07,189 --> 00:00:10,321
weighing about six hundred and sixty tonnes.

5
00:00:10,501 --> 00:00:11,917
It is not decoration.

6
00:00:12,097 --> 00:00:13,084
It swings.

7
00:00:13,264 --> 00:00:15,354
When a typhoon hits Taiwan,

8
00:00:15,354 --> 00:00:18,605
the ball moves against the building's sway

9
00:00:18,605 --> 00:00:19,998
and calms it down.

10
00:00:20,178 --> 00:00:23,150
Today, eleven parts of a skyscraper,

11
00:00:23,150 --> 00:00:25,131
and why each one exists,

12
00:00:25,131 --> 00:00:27,608
starting with that giant ball.

13
00:00:27,959 --> 00:00:29,941
Skyscrapers look rigid,

14
00:00:29,941 --> 00:00:31,407
but they are not.

15
00:00:31,587 --> 00:00:33,282
They bend in the wind,

16
00:00:33,282 --> 00:00:35,286
they shake in earthquakes,

17
00:00:35,286 --> 00:00:37,907
and their tops move back and forth

18
00:00:37,907 --> 00:00:39,833
every day by centimeters,

19
00:00:39,833 --> 00:00:41,606
sometimes by much more.

20
00:00:41,786 --> 00:00:44,194
The real enemy of a tall building

21
00:00:44,194 --> 00:00:46,384
is usually not its own weight.

22
00:00:46,564 --> 00:00:47,504
It is the wind.

23
00:00:47,854 --> 00:00:50,676
As always, every fact comes from a

24
00:00:50,676 --> 00:00:53,166
named source, building owners,

25
00:00:53,166 --> 00:00:56,486
engineering firms, and official reports,

26
00:00:56,486 --> 00:00:58,976
all listed in the description.

27
00:00:59,156 --> 00:01:00,781
Let's start with the ball.

28
00:01:01,132 --> 00:01:03,437
Why does Taipei one oh one have

29
00:01:03,437 --> 00:01:06,039
a six hundred and sixty tonne steel

30
00:01:06,039 --> 00:01:07,749
ball hanging inside it?

31
00:01:07,929 --> 00:01:10,100
Because it fights the sway.

32
00:01:10,280 --> 00:01:12,369
The ball hangs from cables,

33
00:01:12,369 --> 00:01:15,773
between the eighty seventh and ninety second

34
00:01:15,773 --> 00:01:18,094
floors, like a giant pendulum.

35
00:01:18,274 --> 00:01:20,302
It is tuned so that when the

36
00:01:20,302 --> 00:01:22,693
wind pushes the building one way,

37
00:01:22,693 --> 00:01:25,446
the ball's inertia makes it lag behind

38
00:01:25,446 --> 00:01:27,113
and pull the other way,

39
00:01:27,113 --> 00:01:29,431
taking energy out of the motion.

40
00:01:29,780 --> 00:01:33,055
Hydraulic dampers under the ball absorb that

41
00:01:33,055 --> 00:01:35,214
energy and turn it into heat.

42
00:01:35,394 --> 00:01:38,157
Engineers say the ball can reduce the

43
00:01:38,157 --> 00:01:40,845
building's sway by up to about forty

44
00:01:40,845 --> 00:01:41,443
percent.

45
00:01:41,623 --> 00:01:43,509
In two thousand fifteen,

46
00:01:43,509 --> 00:01:45,395
during Typhoon Soudelor,

47
00:01:45,395 --> 00:01:47,203
it swung about a meter,

48
00:01:47,203 --> 00:01:50,504
the biggest movement ever recorded for it.

49
00:01:50,855 --> 00:01:53,258
Most buildings hide their dampers.

50
00:01:53,438 --> 00:01:56,524
Taipei one oh one turned its damper

51
00:01:56,524 --> 00:01:58,817
into a tourist attraction,

52
00:01:58,817 --> 00:02:02,610
painted gold, with its own cartoon mascots.

53
00:02:02,790 --> 00:02:05,367
Visitors can walk around it and watch

54
00:02:05,367 --> 00:02:08,432
the counterweight that keeps the tower calm.

55
00:02:08,782 --> 00:02:11,671
Why are skyscrapers allowed to sway at

56
00:02:11,671 --> 00:02:11,975
all?

57
00:02:12,155 --> 00:02:14,930
Because a building that cannot bend would

58
00:02:14,930 --> 00:02:15,336
break.

59
00:02:15,516 --> 00:02:18,667
Steel and concrete flex a little under

60
00:02:18,667 --> 00:02:21,901
the wind, and that flexibility lets the

61
00:02:21,901 --> 00:02:24,305
building absorb gusts safely.

62
00:02:24,485 --> 00:02:26,530
The top of a very tall tower

63
00:02:26,530 --> 00:02:29,013
can move by tens of centimeters in

64
00:02:29,013 --> 00:02:31,787
a storm, and the structure is designed

65
00:02:31,787 --> 00:02:32,299
for it.

66
00:02:32,648 --> 00:02:34,952
The real limit is not strength,

67
00:02:34,952 --> 00:02:35,992
it is comfort.

68
00:02:36,172 --> 00:02:39,259
People feel motion long before the building

69
00:02:39,259 --> 00:02:40,479
is in any danger.

70
00:02:40,659 --> 00:02:43,315
Water in toilets sloshes,

71
00:02:43,315 --> 00:02:47,776
lamps swing, and some people feel seasick.

72
00:02:47,956 --> 00:02:50,883
So engineers set limits on how much

73
00:02:50,883 --> 00:02:53,391
the top floors may accelerate,

74
00:02:53,391 --> 00:02:57,070
especially in apartments where people sleep.

75
00:02:57,420 --> 00:03:00,203
Tall buildings are tested as models in

76
00:03:00,203 --> 00:03:02,765
wind tunnels before they are built,

77
00:03:02,765 --> 00:03:04,888
often in a model of the whole

78
00:03:04,888 --> 00:03:06,133
city around them,

79
00:03:06,133 --> 00:03:09,500
because neighboring towers change how the wind

80
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:09,866
hits.

81
00:03:10,046 --> 00:03:13,236
The design is then adjusted until the

82
00:03:13,236 --> 00:03:15,735
motion at the top feels calm.

83
00:03:16,085 --> 00:03:19,397
Why does the Burj Khalifa get narrower

84
00:03:19,397 --> 00:03:21,227
in steps as it rises,

85
00:03:21,227 --> 00:03:23,319
like a spiral staircase?

86
00:03:23,499 --> 00:03:25,751
Because it confuses the wind.

87
00:03:25,931 --> 00:03:29,161
When wind flows around a tall building,

88
00:03:29,161 --> 00:03:32,060
it sheds swirls of air on alternate

89
00:03:32,060 --> 00:03:33,965
sides, called vortices.

90
00:03:34,145 --> 00:03:36,433
If those swirls form in a steady

91
00:03:36,433 --> 00:03:38,936
rhythm, they push the tower side to

92
00:03:38,936 --> 00:03:41,438
side, and the building can start to

93
00:03:41,438 --> 00:03:41,796
rock.

94
00:03:42,145 --> 00:03:45,688
The Burj Khalifa's shape changes every few

95
00:03:45,688 --> 00:03:46,279
floors.

96
00:03:46,459 --> 00:03:48,737
Its Y shaped plan steps back in

97
00:03:48,737 --> 00:03:50,868
a spiral, so the width of the

98
00:03:50,868 --> 00:03:53,587
building is different at each height.

99
00:03:53,767 --> 00:03:57,210
The swirls cannot organize into one strong

100
00:03:57,210 --> 00:03:57,784
rhythm.

101
00:03:57,964 --> 00:04:01,064
Engineers called it confusing the wind,

102
00:04:01,064 --> 00:04:04,004
and they tested many versions in wind

103
00:04:04,004 --> 00:04:04,640
tunnels.

104
00:04:04,989 --> 00:04:07,428
The building was also turned on its

105
00:04:07,428 --> 00:04:09,866
site to face the strongest winds in

106
00:04:09,866 --> 00:04:11,189
the best direction.

107
00:04:11,369 --> 00:04:14,170
At eight hundred and twenty eight meters,

108
00:04:14,170 --> 00:04:16,835
the tallest building on Earth is shaped

109
00:04:16,835 --> 00:04:19,090
less by architecture than by air.

110
00:04:19,440 --> 00:04:22,244
Why does the Shanghai Tower twist as

111
00:04:22,244 --> 00:04:22,946
it rises?

112
00:04:23,126 --> 00:04:26,133
Because the twist cuts the wind load.

113
00:04:26,313 --> 00:04:28,917
The tower rotates by about a hundred

114
00:04:28,917 --> 00:04:31,666
and twenty degrees from bottom to top,

115
00:04:31,666 --> 00:04:33,836
with a rounded triangle shape.

116
00:04:34,016 --> 00:04:36,847
Wind tunnel tests showed that this twist

117
00:04:36,847 --> 00:04:39,537
reduces the wind loads on the building

118
00:04:39,537 --> 00:04:41,802
by about a quarter compared to a

119
00:04:41,802 --> 00:04:43,501
plain rectangular tower.

120
00:04:43,852 --> 00:04:46,705
Less wind load means less steel and

121
00:04:46,705 --> 00:04:47,439
concrete.

122
00:04:47,619 --> 00:04:50,386
The designers said it saved tens of

123
00:04:50,386 --> 00:04:52,994
millions of dollars in structure.

124
00:04:53,174 --> 00:04:56,097
At six hundred and thirty two meters,

125
00:04:56,097 --> 00:04:58,940
it is the tallest building in China,

126
00:04:58,940 --> 00:05:01,231
and the twist also gives it a

127
00:05:01,231 --> 00:05:04,391
glass skin with spaces between the inner

128
00:05:04,391 --> 00:05:05,654
and outer walls,

129
00:05:05,654 --> 00:05:07,234
used as sky gardens.

130
00:05:07,585 --> 00:05:11,524
Twisting, tapering, and cutting holes through the

131
00:05:11,524 --> 00:05:14,580
top are now common tricks in supertall

132
00:05:14,580 --> 00:05:15,143
design.

133
00:05:15,323 --> 00:05:17,227
They all do the same thing.

134
00:05:17,407 --> 00:05:19,772
They stop the wind from finding a

135
00:05:19,772 --> 00:05:21,923
steady rhythm to push against.

136
00:05:22,273 --> 00:05:25,194
Why was a New York skyscraper secretly

137
00:05:25,194 --> 00:05:28,577
repaired at night in nineteen seventy eight?

138
00:05:28,757 --> 00:05:31,414
The building was the Citicorp Center,

139
00:05:31,414 --> 00:05:34,288
famous for standing on four tall columns

140
00:05:34,288 --> 00:05:36,227
at the middle of each side,

141
00:05:36,227 --> 00:05:37,592
not at the corners.

142
00:05:37,772 --> 00:05:39,026
After it opened,

143
00:05:39,026 --> 00:05:41,612
a question from a student led the

144
00:05:41,612 --> 00:05:44,825
structural engineer, William LeMessurier,

145
00:05:44,825 --> 00:05:47,881
to recheck his design for winds hitting

146
00:05:47,881 --> 00:05:49,762
the building diagonally,

147
00:05:49,762 --> 00:05:50,938
at its corners.

148
00:05:51,288 --> 00:05:53,122
He found a serious problem.

149
00:05:53,302 --> 00:05:56,195
The diagonal braces had been joined with

150
00:05:56,195 --> 00:05:57,859
bolts instead of welds,

151
00:05:57,859 --> 00:06:00,824
and under certain winds those joints were

152
00:06:00,824 --> 00:06:01,475
too weak.

153
00:06:01,655 --> 00:06:04,874
His calculations suggested that a strong storm

154
00:06:04,874 --> 00:06:06,973
could bring the building down.

155
00:06:07,153 --> 00:06:09,208
Hurricane season was coming.

156
00:06:09,388 --> 00:06:10,385
So, at night,

157
00:06:10,385 --> 00:06:13,684
welders covered the joints with thick steel

158
00:06:13,684 --> 00:06:17,289
plates while offices worked normally during the

159
00:06:17,289 --> 00:06:17,596
day.

160
00:06:17,946 --> 00:06:21,004
Emergency plans were made to evacuate the

161
00:06:21,004 --> 00:06:23,913
neighborhood if a hurricane approached.

162
00:06:24,093 --> 00:06:27,117
The repairs were finished before any storm

163
00:06:27,117 --> 00:06:29,637
tested them, and the public did not

164
00:06:29,637 --> 00:06:32,661
learn the full story until nineteen ninety

165
00:06:32,661 --> 00:06:33,021
five.

166
00:06:33,201 --> 00:06:35,954
The building also has a tuned mass

167
00:06:35,954 --> 00:06:37,087
damper on top,

168
00:06:37,087 --> 00:06:39,759
a block of concrete of about four

169
00:06:39,759 --> 00:06:42,269
hundred tonnes, sliding on oil.

170
00:06:42,619 --> 00:06:45,196
Why do most skyscrapers have a massive

171
00:06:45,196 --> 00:06:47,773
concrete tube running up their middle?

172
00:06:47,953 --> 00:06:49,143
It is the core,

173
00:06:49,143 --> 00:06:50,809
the building's spine.

174
00:06:50,989 --> 00:06:52,781
It holds the elevators,

175
00:06:52,781 --> 00:06:54,729
stairs, pipes and cables,

176
00:06:54,729 --> 00:06:57,300
and it is the main structure that

177
00:06:57,300 --> 00:07:00,027
stops the tower from bending in the

178
00:07:00,027 --> 00:07:00,417
wind.

179
00:07:00,597 --> 00:07:03,216
Thick concrete walls on all sides of

180
00:07:03,216 --> 00:07:05,252
the core make it very stiff.

181
00:07:05,603 --> 00:07:07,408
On a construction site,

182
00:07:07,408 --> 00:07:09,684
the core usually rises first,

183
00:07:09,684 --> 00:07:12,824
several floors ahead of everything else.

184
00:07:13,004 --> 00:07:16,317
Special climbing forms are pushed up a

185
00:07:16,317 --> 00:07:18,148
few meters at a time,

186
00:07:18,148 --> 00:07:21,723
and concrete is poured around steel bars.

187
00:07:21,903 --> 00:07:24,922
The floors and outer columns follow behind.

188
00:07:25,272 --> 00:07:27,837
The core is also the safe zone.

189
00:07:28,017 --> 00:07:31,222
Stairs inside it are protected by fire

190
00:07:31,222 --> 00:07:35,102
resistant walls and pressurized with fresh air

191
00:07:35,102 --> 00:07:36,620
to keep smoke out.

192
00:07:36,800 --> 00:07:39,850
Some towers in Asia also have refuge

193
00:07:39,850 --> 00:07:42,222
floors every so many levels,

194
00:07:42,222 --> 00:07:45,611
open floors where people can wait safely

195
00:07:45,611 --> 00:07:46,797
during a fire.

196
00:07:47,147 --> 00:07:50,468
Why do some skyscrapers have entire floors

197
00:07:50,468 --> 00:07:52,998
filled with giant steel trusses?

198
00:07:53,178 --> 00:07:54,397
They are outriggers.

199
00:07:54,577 --> 00:07:57,163
A core alone would bend like a

200
00:07:57,163 --> 00:07:59,233
fishing rod in the wind.

201
00:07:59,413 --> 00:08:02,293
Outriggers are huge braces that connect the

202
00:08:02,293 --> 00:08:04,571
core to the columns on the outside

203
00:08:04,571 --> 00:08:05,642
of the building,

204
00:08:05,642 --> 00:08:07,719
so the whole width of the tower

205
00:08:07,719 --> 00:08:08,724
works together.

206
00:08:09,074 --> 00:08:11,540
When the wind pushes the tower,

207
00:08:11,540 --> 00:08:13,369
the core tries to bend.

208
00:08:13,549 --> 00:08:16,545
The outriggers turn that bending into pushing

209
00:08:16,545 --> 00:08:18,743
and pulling on the outer columns,

210
00:08:18,743 --> 00:08:21,273
which are far apart and very effective

211
00:08:21,273 --> 00:08:22,338
at resisting it.

212
00:08:22,518 --> 00:08:24,877
It is like a person spreading their

213
00:08:24,877 --> 00:08:26,698
arms to keep their balance.

214
00:08:27,048 --> 00:08:30,613
Outrigger floors are usually used for machinery,

215
00:08:30,613 --> 00:08:33,138
because the trusses get in the way

216
00:08:33,138 --> 00:08:33,955
of offices.

217
00:08:34,135 --> 00:08:36,361
If you see a tower with dark

218
00:08:36,361 --> 00:08:38,666
bands every few dozen floors,

219
00:08:38,666 --> 00:08:42,482
those bands often hide outriggers and mechanical

220
00:08:42,482 --> 00:08:42,959
rooms.

221
00:08:43,309 --> 00:08:45,637
Why do you have to change elevators

222
00:08:45,637 --> 00:08:47,767
halfway up a very tall building?

223
00:08:47,947 --> 00:08:50,744
Because elevators take up space,

224
00:08:50,744 --> 00:08:52,405
and space is money.

225
00:08:52,585 --> 00:08:55,378
If every elevator went from the ground

226
00:08:55,378 --> 00:08:56,480
to every floor,

227
00:08:56,480 --> 00:08:59,053
the bottom of the building would be

228
00:08:59,053 --> 00:09:01,037
almost all elevator shafts.

229
00:09:01,217 --> 00:09:05,214
So supertall towers use express elevators to

230
00:09:05,214 --> 00:09:08,666
sky lobbies, where you change to local

231
00:09:08,666 --> 00:09:11,936
elevators that serve only a group of

232
00:09:11,936 --> 00:09:12,572
floors.

233
00:09:12,922 --> 00:09:14,361
There is another limit.

234
00:09:14,541 --> 00:09:17,470
Steel elevator ropes get heavier the longer

235
00:09:17,470 --> 00:09:19,650
they are, and at some point they

236
00:09:19,650 --> 00:09:21,693
mostly carry their own weight.

237
00:09:21,873 --> 00:09:24,791
That limited a single elevator rise to

238
00:09:24,791 --> 00:09:26,865
around five hundred meters.

239
00:09:27,045 --> 00:09:30,364
Newer carbon fiber ropes are much lighter,

240
00:09:30,364 --> 00:09:33,446
and could allow elevators to rise twice

241
00:09:33,446 --> 00:09:34,000
as far.

242
00:09:34,350 --> 00:09:37,774
Some buildings use double deck elevators,

243
00:09:37,774 --> 00:09:40,531
two cabins stacked on top of each

244
00:09:40,531 --> 00:09:43,370
other, serving two floors at once.

245
00:09:43,550 --> 00:09:47,178
Fast elevators also control air pressure in

246
00:09:47,178 --> 00:09:50,300
the cabin, so passengers' ears do not

247
00:09:50,300 --> 00:09:53,421
hurt as they climb hundreds of meters

248
00:09:53,421 --> 00:09:54,940
in under a minute.

249
00:09:55,290 --> 00:09:58,196
Why do skyscrapers have red or white

250
00:09:58,196 --> 00:10:00,619
lights blinking on their tops?

251
00:10:00,799 --> 00:10:02,946
They are aircraft warning lights.

252
00:10:03,126 --> 00:10:05,555
Any structure tall enough to be a

253
00:10:05,555 --> 00:10:08,572
hazard for planes and helicopters must be

254
00:10:08,572 --> 00:10:10,853
marked, so pilots can see it at

255
00:10:10,853 --> 00:10:12,693
night and in bad weather.

256
00:10:12,873 --> 00:10:16,626
Aviation authorities set the rules for color,

257
00:10:16,626 --> 00:10:18,794
brightness and flash rate.

258
00:10:19,144 --> 00:10:22,340
Very tall structures often use bright white

259
00:10:22,340 --> 00:10:25,164
flashing lights during the day and red

260
00:10:25,164 --> 00:10:26,353
lights at night.

261
00:10:26,533 --> 00:10:28,893
The lights are placed at the top

262
00:10:28,893 --> 00:10:31,179
and at levels along the height,

263
00:10:31,179 --> 00:10:33,907
so the whole outline of the structure

264
00:10:33,907 --> 00:10:34,792
can be seen,

265
00:10:34,792 --> 00:10:36,193
not just one point.

266
00:10:36,543 --> 00:10:38,888
Changing a bulb at the top of

267
00:10:38,888 --> 00:10:41,638
a skyscraper can be a climbing job

268
00:10:41,638 --> 00:10:42,528
of its own,

269
00:10:42,528 --> 00:10:45,358
which is why modern lights use long

270
00:10:45,358 --> 00:10:46,167
life LEDs.

271
00:10:46,347 --> 00:10:47,858
And in some cities,

272
00:10:47,858 --> 00:10:50,800
people use the blinking red lights to

273
00:10:50,800 --> 00:10:52,629
tell the time of night,

274
00:10:52,629 --> 00:10:55,173
the weather, or simply as a sign

275
00:10:55,173 --> 00:10:55,809
of home.

276
00:10:56,160 --> 00:10:58,423
Why is there a small crane on

277
00:10:58,423 --> 00:11:00,687
the roof of many skyscrapers?

278
00:11:00,867 --> 00:11:03,247
It is a building maintenance unit.

279
00:11:03,427 --> 00:11:05,794
It moves around the roof on rails

280
00:11:05,794 --> 00:11:07,371
and lowers a platform,

281
00:11:07,371 --> 00:11:08,591
called a gondola,

282
00:11:08,591 --> 00:11:10,742
down the face of the building.

283
00:11:10,922 --> 00:11:13,379
Workers ride it to clean windows,

284
00:11:13,379 --> 00:11:16,505
replace broken glass and inspect the outer

285
00:11:16,505 --> 00:11:16,878
skin.

286
00:11:17,228 --> 00:11:18,868
On a supertall tower,

287
00:11:18,868 --> 00:11:21,836
cleaning all the windows once can take

288
00:11:21,836 --> 00:11:22,382
months.

289
00:11:22,562 --> 00:11:25,279
The Burj Khalifa has several of these

290
00:11:25,279 --> 00:11:28,583
machines, some hidden inside the building and

291
00:11:28,583 --> 00:11:30,271
rolled out when needed.

292
00:11:30,451 --> 00:11:33,563
Window cleaners there work hundreds of meters

293
00:11:33,563 --> 00:11:34,739
above the ground,

294
00:11:34,739 --> 00:11:36,814
often in strong wind and heat.

295
00:11:37,164 --> 00:11:39,448
The roof machine is part of the

296
00:11:39,448 --> 00:11:41,069
design from the start,

297
00:11:41,069 --> 00:11:43,868
because every shape on the facade must

298
00:11:43,868 --> 00:11:44,826
be reachable.

299
00:11:45,006 --> 00:11:47,896
Fancy curves and sloping glass may look

300
00:11:47,896 --> 00:11:50,712
great, but somebody has to clean them,

301
00:11:50,712 --> 00:11:53,009
and the gondola must be able to

302
00:11:53,009 --> 00:11:53,528
follow.

303
00:11:53,878 --> 00:11:56,638
What holds up a building weighing hundreds

304
00:11:56,638 --> 00:11:58,150
of thousands of tonnes?

305
00:11:58,330 --> 00:12:01,302
Foundations that reach deep underground.

306
00:12:01,482 --> 00:12:04,145
The Burj Khalifa stands on a thick

307
00:12:04,145 --> 00:12:07,592
concrete raft supported by about two hundred

308
00:12:07,592 --> 00:12:10,412
concrete piles, each about one and a

309
00:12:10,412 --> 00:12:13,232
half meters wide and more than forty

310
00:12:13,232 --> 00:12:14,172
meters long.

311
00:12:14,352 --> 00:12:16,713
The ground under Dubai is not hard

312
00:12:16,713 --> 00:12:19,213
rock, so the piles hold the building

313
00:12:19,213 --> 00:12:21,852
mostly by friction along their length.

314
00:12:22,202 --> 00:12:25,843
The groundwater in Dubai is also salty

315
00:12:25,843 --> 00:12:29,388
and aggressive to concrete and steel.

316
00:12:29,568 --> 00:12:33,236
So the foundation used special dense concrete

317
00:12:33,236 --> 00:12:36,171
and a system that protects the steel

318
00:12:36,171 --> 00:12:37,964
inside from corrosion,

319
00:12:37,964 --> 00:12:41,143
the same way ships protect their hulls.

320
00:12:41,494 --> 00:12:44,917
Foundations are the least glamorous part of

321
00:12:44,917 --> 00:12:45,952
a skyscraper.

322
00:12:46,132 --> 00:12:47,531
Nobody visits them,

323
00:12:47,531 --> 00:12:50,551
and nobody photographs them once they are

324
00:12:50,551 --> 00:12:51,066
buried.

325
00:12:51,246 --> 00:12:53,877
But every meter of height above ground

326
00:12:53,877 --> 00:12:56,231
depends on what was done below it,

327
00:12:56,231 --> 00:12:58,723
before the tower even began to rise.

328
00:12:59,073 --> 00:13:01,058
So here are the eleven.

329
00:13:01,238 --> 00:13:04,154
A giant ball that fights the sway,

330
00:13:04,154 --> 00:13:06,556
and a top that is allowed to

331
00:13:06,556 --> 00:13:06,985
move.

332
00:13:07,165 --> 00:13:09,413
Steps that confuse the wind,

333
00:13:09,413 --> 00:13:11,581
a twist that cuts the load,

334
00:13:11,581 --> 00:13:13,829
and a tower fixed in secret.

335
00:13:14,009 --> 00:13:15,469
A concrete spine,

336
00:13:15,469 --> 00:13:18,646
steel arms reaching out to the edges,

337
00:13:18,646 --> 00:13:21,394
elevators you change halfway up,

338
00:13:21,394 --> 00:13:23,283
red lights for pilots,

339
00:13:23,283 --> 00:13:26,031
a crane that cleans the windows,

340
00:13:26,031 --> 00:13:28,521
and piles deep in the ground.

341
00:13:28,872 --> 00:13:31,112
Next time you stand at the top

342
00:13:31,112 --> 00:13:32,531
of a tall building,

343
00:13:32,531 --> 00:13:35,220
remember that it is moving with you,

344
00:13:35,220 --> 00:13:36,639
exactly as planned.

345
00:13:36,819 --> 00:13:39,918
If you want more machines and structures

346
00:13:39,918 --> 00:13:41,700
explained part by part,

347
00:13:41,700 --> 00:13:43,947
subscribe to Machine Reverse.

348
00:13:44,127 --> 00:13:45,908
And tell us in the comments what

349
00:13:45,908 --> 00:13:47,633
you want us to take apart next.
